The impact of infrared renormalons in the color dipole gluon distribution's variance is being quantified, and calculations demonstrate how non-linear small-x saturation impacts shift the Borel plane's initial IR pole from $2/\beta_2$ to $1/\beta_2$, thereby changing the estimated uncertainty to approximately $\Lambda_{\rm QCD}^2$ instead of $\Lambda_{\rm QCD}^4$. Moreover, the influence of infrared renormalons is found to be insignificant when considering the CKM-averaged total charm cross-section at mid-rapidity in deep inelastic scattering at LHC energies. Nevertheless, the proposed method can be applied to investigate the impact of infrared renormalons on other quantities of interest. Moreover, the framework is utilized to formulate a minimum-balance criterion for resummation validity in the presence of infrared renormalons. The significance of accounting for the color dipole gluon distribution's variance is highlighted through a detailed analysis of the CKM-averaged total charm cross-section cross-section at LHC energies. Lastly, the implications of the proposed study are explored within the context of the color dipole gluon distribution's generalization to multiple radii. The associated code is publicly accessible online. 

